{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5","id":"msg_011CegoS2pz76SLUciabq7DX","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"Takes two nested mappings and builds a new dict from them, letting `overlay` win wherever the two disagree. The merge is recursive, but under one condition only: when both sides hold a `dict` at the same key, those two dicts are merged; in every other case the overlay's value simply takes the base's place.\n\nThat single rule accounts for the rest of the behaviour. Lists are not concatenated and sets are not unioned, so a one-element list in the overlay displaces a ten-element list in the base. `None` in the overlay is an ordinary value like any other: it overwrites, rather than signalling that the base entry should be left alone. Nothing the overlay can say will delete a key. And when the two sides disagree about shape — a `dict` in the base where the overlay has a scalar, or the reverse — the losing subtree goes as a whole, with no attempt at a partial merge.\n\nNeither argument is modified, but the new dict is only shallowly independent of them. Any value that wasn't itself merged as a `dict` is the same object in the result as in the input it came from, so a nested list or a custom object mutated after the call shows that change through both. If the inputs outlive the call, treat the result as read-only or deep-copy it.\n\nRecursion is narrower than it may appear, too: only genuine `dict` instances trigger it. A `Mapping` implementation that isn't a `dict` subclass — the ordered and sorted dictionary types other libraries offer, for instance — counts as an opaque value and is replaced wholesale. Depth of recursion follows how deeply the inputs are nested, and a self-referential structure will never terminate."}],"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":9062,"cache_read_input_tokens":10475,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_toke